<!-- QUARRY:PART:output_schema -->
Return a JSON object with these keys:

"entry_points": list of {repo, file, function, kind} objects. Include EVERY entry
point you found. Allowed kind values: http_handler, cli_arg, main, fuzz_harness,
library_export, message_handler, unknown. Use "." for repo when you have no other
identifier. "file" must be a path you actually read; "function" the real symbol.

"responsibility": one-sentence description of what this subsystem does, based on
the code you read.

"notes": a per-class inventory of candidate sinks and input sources for the
hunters, as free text with these headed buckets (cite file:line for each entry;
write "(none observed)" under a bucket you found nothing for). If entry_points is
empty, this field MUST also justify it file by file.
  Input vectors: (request params, headers, body fields, path/query params, CLI args, env, file/message payloads)
  SSRF sinks: (outbound HTTP clients, fetch/url openers, webhooks, redirect followers, proxy passthroughs)
  Injection sinks (SQL/command): (raw/string-built queries, ORM raw/execute, subprocess/exec/shell)
  XSS sinks: (HTML/JS render points — innerHTML, template raw/|safe, string-built HTML responses)
  IDOR / object-access points: (endpoints that fetch/update/delete a resource by a user-supplied id)
  Secrets / config: (hardcoded credentials, connection strings, config/env with sensitive values)

"tool_calls": list of tool calls to run next. Put a tool call here for EVERY file
or directory you still need to inspect. Each item is
{"tool": "<list_dir|read_file|grep|search_code>", "inputs": {...}}. Return [] ONLY
when you have read every source file and recorded every entry point.

"proposed_actions": optional provenance for the tool calls above; the tool_calls
list is what actually runs. When present, each entry must have:
  - kind: the action kind (e.g. "read_file", "list_dir")
  - tool_name: the tool name (same as kind for most tools)
  - args: dict of tool arguments
  - reasoning: object with FOUR required fields:
      - hypothesis: what you believe about this subsystem and are testing (≥4 words)
      - target_ref: a CONCRETE path — e.g. "src/routes/index.js" or "src/controllers/"
      - expected_evidence: the specific thing you expect to find (NOT "find entry points")
      - why_this_tool: why this tool reveals the subsystem's entry points or structure

If — and only if — you have read every file and recorded every entry point and
the sink/source inventory, return with tool_calls=[] and proposed_actions=[].
